wlan: optional STP/BTIF transport for the ROM init-cmd command set

Adds the experiment asked for in M83: send the firmware-download commands of
the ROM init-cmd protocol over the STP link (channel 3, WIFI_TASK_INDX) that
wmt_drv already uses for the BTIF ROMv4 patch download, instead of the
AHB/MCIF rings the driver uses today.

New files:

  include/t1100_stp_hif.h        transport selection + 4-call API
  os/linux/t1100_stp_hif.c       STP send/receive on channel 3

  - t1100_hif=0 (default) keeps the vendor path byte for byte: the helpers in
    wlan_lib.c just call nicTxInitCmd()/nicRxWaitResponse()/
    nicTxAcquireResource().
  - t1100_hif=1 routes only the firmware-download command set
    (wlanImageSectionConfig, wlanImageSectionDownload, wlanImageQueryStatus,
    wlanImageSectionDownloadStatus, wlanConfigWifiFunc, plus the diagnostic
    ACCESS_REG read of 0020) through
    mtk_wcn_stp_send_data()/mtk_wcn_stp_receive_data(); the frame bytes are
    unchanged (INIT_HIF_TX_HEADER_T + payload).  The MCIF TX page accounting
    is skipped on that path (there is no TX ring behind STP; the STP link has
    its own window flow control).
  - the RX side registers an event callback, which is also what makes the STP
    core enqueue channel-3 packets at all
    (stp_exp.c:mtk_wcn_sys_check_function_status()).
  - the runtime command path is untouched, and so is the MCIF patch
    experiment: t1100_rom_patch_mcif is skipped when STP is selected (there
    the patch set is wmt_drv's job).

Why this is only a probe: the data path (TX/RX rings, WFDMA, WHISR/WHDR
interrupts) and the "ready" bit (WCIR_WLAN_READY) exist only in the MCIF
block, and the stock 4.14 wlan_drv_gen3.ko imports no STP symbol at all.  If
the MCU answers at all on channel 3 that is the interesting result; a working
netdev cannot come out of it.  See docs/wifi-port-log.md M83.

